## Counting lemma

Fix a non-self-mirror pattern pair `{p,rho_P(p)}`. Let the 14,174-cell universe contain `U` cells on each side. Let the current exact exclusion counts be `e_p` and `e_r`. Reflection closure of the excluded set has at least

\[
\max(e_p,e_r)
\]

excluded cells on each side. Relative to the current exclusion list, this forces at least

\[
2\max(e_p,e_r)-(e_p+e_r)=|e_p-e_r|
\]

additional exclusions.

Equivalently, because `e_p=U-s_p` and `e_r=U-s_r`, where `s_p,s_r` are current survivor counts, the forced gain is

\[
|s_p-s_r|.
\]

Applying this to the current census gives

```text
pair       current survivors   forced new
145/347          4 / 4              0
156/237         16 / 16             0
235/356        654 / 655            1
236/256       1278 / 1204          74
245/346        474 / 479            5
```

The self-mirror pattern `345` contributes no lower bound from this aggregate argument. Therefore

\[
1+74+5=\boxed{80}
\]

additional exclusions are forced, and

\[
4805-80=\boxed{4725}.
\]

## Why the bound is conservative

The argument uses only ground-pattern cardinalities. It does not identify reflected labelled states, so it cannot detect overlap structure within a pattern pair or any new exclusions in the self-mirror `345` class. It also discards the reflection-preserved missing-graph type and all finer state data.

A graph-by-pattern diagnostic on only the 139 most recent two-face exclusions shows a larger imbalance, but using that to certify a stronger global count would require a state-level audit of the intermediate endpoints. That stronger number is intentionally not claimed here.

The next exact bookkeeping target is therefore clear: construct the reflection map on the full 14,174-state representation, map every currently excluded labelled state, and take the exact union. That will turn this aggregate `<=4725` bound into an exact reflection-closed census. After that, pair-face discovery should run only one representative from each reflection orbit of physical face pairs.

